# libstr89

Owning, validated UTF-8 strings for strict ISO C89. `libstr89` supplies the
string/storage layer: validated views, owning finalized strings, mutable
builders, byte-offset editing, and exact byte comparison/search. Every
Unicode fact comes from the sibling `libu89`.

```text
              libu89
             ^      ^
            /        \
      libstr89        \
            ^          \
             \          \
              libj89 ----+   (JSON strings, keys, \uXXXX syntax)
```

A `str89` string is an exact sequence of Unicode scalar values encoded as
well-formed UTF-8, preserving the original byte representation:

- explicit byte length, embedded U+0000 allowed;
- no `strlen()` semantics, no trailing-NUL requirement;
- no normalization, case folding, locale, or collation;
- byte-exact equality; UTF-8 byte offsets;
- editing only at Unicode scalar boundaries.

## Types

| Type | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `str89_view` | borrowed validated UTF-8 bytes |
| `str89` | owning finalized UTF-8 bytes |
| `str89_buf` | owning mutable UTF-8 builder |

Allocation is external: `str89_alloc` callbacks are supplied by the owner and
are never stored inside a value; a NULL allocator means libc.

## Build and test

- `just build` — build `build/libstr89.a` (builds `../libu89` first).
- `just smoke` — one end-to-end path.
- `just unit` — unit programs, one per concern.
- `just fault` — allocation-failure and checked-arithmetic tests.
- `just model` — deterministic reference-model sequences.
- `just corpus` — UTF-8 validity corpus differential against `libu89`.
- `just guard` — guard-page tests (POSIX; not part of the green gate).
- `just cpp-check` — C++23 header compile checks.
- `just test` — smoke, unit, fault, model, corpus.
- `just matrix` — gcc/clang x C89/C23 behavioral matrix.
- `just sanitize` — ASan + UBSan run of the fast suite.
- `just check` — green gate plus the no-Unicode-codec audit.

## Non-goals

`strlen`-style and NUL-terminated-string APIs, locale, ASCII classification,
Unicode properties, normalization, case folding, collation, grapheme/word
segmentation, display width, formatting, numeric conversion, regex,
tokenization, split/join, JSON escaping, UTF-16/UTF-32 storage, and general
byte buffers. Those belong to companion libraries or to callers.
